    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3ef5c851-77d2-46af-9a22-e2bfadd01829",
      "udi": "umb://element/b6ca11cf77404d5097cc369c612c1c58",
      "title": "Concrete Conditioning",
      "content": "<p>In a soil laboratory, establishing the optimal conditions for testing is essential.</p>\n<p>Like all testing, sample conditioning follows step-by-step methodologies to ensure consistency of results.</p>\n<p>Soil conditioning or preparation can take up to 24 hours.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>How are samples conditioned?</strong><br />Samples may be riffled, dried, sieved, soaked, heated, hydrated or compacted for a range of tests.</p>\n<p>Soil samples are prepared using various techniques as required for testing such as Maximum Dry Density (MDD), Optimum Moisture Content (OMC), California Bearing Ratio (CBR), Unconfined Compressive Strength (UCS) or Indirect Tensile Strength (ITS).</p>",
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "9a989a8c-d0dd-494f-9377-272b2c733427",
          "mediaKey": "3962733b-b270-4f98-8c63-4932b5ddc46a",
          "crops": [],
          "focalPoint": {
            "left": 0.5,
            "top": 0.5
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "3ef5c851-77d2-46af-9a22-e2bfadd01829",
      "udi": "umb://element/277eb6f6481b47168dd3dd850158bac8",
      "title": "Concrete Strength",
      "content": "<p>These are the most commonly understood tests among construction professionals.</p>\n<p>These tests provide a comprehensive picture of the soil’s engineering properties, influencing critical decisions in foundation design, road works and major construction.</p>\n<p> </p>\n<p><strong>What does this include?</strong><br />Maximum Dry Density (MDD) refers to the highest achievable density of a soil under specific compaction conditions, the point at which a soil achieves optimal compaction and, therefore, maximum stability.</p>\n<p>Optimum Moisture Content (OMC) represents the moisture level at which a soil attains its maximum density during compaction, a vital parameter indicating the precise moisture required for optimal compaction.</p>\n<p>California Bearing Ratio (CBR) is a key performance indicator for assessing the load-bearing capacity of a soil, crucial in pavement design and to help engineers evaluate the soil’s ability to support heavy loads.</p>\n<p>Unconfined Compressive Strength (UCS) provides insights into the soil’s structural integrity, particularly important in geotechnical engineering and to assess the strength of foundations and retaining walls.</p>\n<p>Indirect Tensile Strength (ITS) is a measure of a soil’s ability to resist tensile forces under indirect loading conditions, providing insights into the soil’s durability and behavior under different stress conditions.</p>",
      "images": [
        {
          "key": "1a0cedce-00c7-4fd8-a58d-db89799eff5c",
          "mediaKey": "9ee868ec-c1f3-423a-a3af-2196a567b032",
          "crops": [],
          "focalPoint": {
            "left": 0.5,
            "top": 0.5
          }
        }
      ]
    },
